comments.body:
> Ok. Start with basics. I think we both agree that under this security model, we'll keep serving content to your 2010 Smart TV with 2010 roots, but nobody is pretending that there's any security there. Agree?

Yes.

> I think we both agree that a fully upgraded RP won't have this split-view problem because either every layer understands and is using the inner cert, or some layer doesn't understand the nested cert and doesn't have the outer TA and so hard-fails (nothing we can do about that).

No, that's not quite the issue. If the other layer hard failed, that would be fine-ish because it's failing closed.

The problem is the other layer is going to *assume the outer certificate is trustworthy when it actually wasn't*. Specifically consider an application that writes:

```
if (!OpenSSL.verify_cert(cert)) {
  error();
}
// OpenSSL said yes, so I can assume that the SPKI
// and SAN pair is legit.
proceed_with(cert.spki, cert.sans):
```

Normally, this application is fine. If the path validator says yes on a chain, you know the target certificate you passed in is valid as can trust the SPKI/SAN pair in it. This is a fine assumption today.

But if OpenSSL is updated to process turduckens, it might have actually validated `cert.inner`, while `cert.sans` and `cert.spki` are under attacker control. This is bad.

The point of the binding is to stop those fields from being under attacker control. Yes, we could demand that every layer of application be updated, but when the failure mode is this bad, I don't think that's acceptable. And I think the outer binding is really not as complicated as you fear it is, so this seems like a small price to pay.
